Chapter: GpuDoorbell

  cites Kernel chapter GpuBridge
  cites AI chapter GpuProxy
  cites AI chapter Tensor
  cites Foreword chapter Maybe

 The compute bridge answers real arithmetic, at a cost that does not
 grow with the operand, and refuses what it cannot serve.

 The bridge used to drive a polled serial line and spend two VM exits on
 every byte in each direction, so a dispatch cost a multiple of the
 operand size: 160 exits for the 2x2 matmul below, 112 for its relu, and
 24,640 for the 32x32. It now builds the command in guest memory and
 rings a doorbell, which is four exits whatever the operand is. Measured
 with CODEX_VM_COM3_STAT.

 THE REFUSAL CASE IS THE CONTROL AND IT IS WHY IT IS HERE. Until the
 serial path was deleted this file ran every case through both
 transports, and the two answers beside each other were what would catch
 a transport that carried the bytes wrongly rather than not at all. With
 one transport left that comparison is gone, so the test has to be able
 to fail some other way: the last case asks for a dispatch the host must
 turn down and requires NONE. A bridge that answered everything, or one
 that answered nothing, fails one half or the other -- which is the
 property a test of a single transport most easily loses.

 The values carry the rest. A*B is [[19,22],[43,50]], four distinct
 numbers, none zero, none equal to an operand, and none producible by
 echoing the input back. The 32x32 case multiplies ones, so every entry
 of the product is 32.0 and no operand appears in the answer either.

 We say:

Section: Operands

  bridge : GpuBridge
  bridge = gpu-bridge-init

  mat-a : Tensor
  mat-a = Tensor { data = [1000, 2000, 3000, 4000], rows = 2, cols = 2 }

  mat-b : Tensor
  mat-b = Tensor { data = [5000, 6000, 7000, 8000], rows = 2, cols = 2 }

  relu-in : Tensor
  relu-in = Tensor { data = [0 - 2000, 3000, 0, 0 - 5000], rows = 2, cols = 2 }

  fill : Integer, Integer, List Integer -> List Integer
  fill (i) (n) (acc) = if i >= n then acc else fill (i + 1) n (list-push acc 1000)

  side : Integer = 32

  square : Tensor
  square = Tensor { data = fill 0 (side * side) [], rows = side, cols = side }

 A tensor whose declared shape and whose actual data disagree. The host
 computes the operand count from the dimensions and checks it against the
 length it was rung with, so this is refused at the boundary rather than
 read off the end of the command buffer. It is also cheap: claiming
 200x200 costs four elements of storage, not forty thousand.

  mis-shaped : Tensor
  mis-shaped = Tensor { data = [1000, 1000, 1000, 1000], rows = 200, cols = 200 }

Section: Tolerance

 The guest decoder divides the mantissa down to scale 1000 before it
 applies the exponent, so it loses low bits on any value above 1.0 --
 19.0 decodes to 18992, not 19000. That is the decoder's precision and
 not the transport's, so these assertions allow one part in a hundred.
 The smallest gap between two entries of the 2x2 product is 3000 in these
 units, nowhere near wide enough to admit a wrong answer.

  near : Integer, Integer -> Boolean
  near (got) (want) =
    let diff = if got > want then got - want else want - got
    in let slack = if want > 0 then want / 100 else 100
    in diff <= slack

Section: Reports

  mm-report : Maybe Tensor -> Text
  mm-report (m) =
    when m
     is None -> "NONE"
     is Just (t) -> mm-values t

  mm-values : Tensor -> Text
  mm-values (t) =
    if list-length (t.data) /= 4 then "count " & show (list-length (t.data))
    else if near (list-at (t.data) 0) 19000
     & near (list-at (t.data) 1) 22000
     & near (list-at (t.data) 2) 43000
     & near (list-at (t.data) 3) 50000
    then "ok"
    else "wrong " & show (list-at (t.data) 0) & " " & show (list-at (t.data) 1) & " "
       & show (list-at (t.data) 2) & " " & show (list-at (t.data) 3)

  relu-report : Maybe Tensor -> Text
  relu-report (m) =
    when m
     is None -> "NONE"
     is Just (t) -> relu-values t

  relu-values : Tensor -> Text
  relu-values (t) =
    if list-length (t.data) /= 4 then "count " & show (list-length (t.data))
    else if near (list-at (t.data) 0) 0 & near (list-at (t.data) 1) 3000
     & near (list-at (t.data) 2) 0 & near (list-at (t.data) 3) 0
    then "ok"
    else "wrong " & show (list-at (t.data) 0) & " " & show (list-at (t.data) 1)

  big-report : Maybe Tensor -> Text
  big-report (m) =
    when m
     is None -> "NONE"
     is Just (t) -> big-values t

  big-values : Tensor -> Text
  big-values (t) =
    if list-length (t.data) /= side * side then "count " & show (list-length (t.data))
    else let v = list-at (t.data) 0
    in if v > 31000 then if v < 33000 then "ok" else "high " & show v
       else "low " & show v

 A refusal is the wanted answer here, so it is reported as such rather
 than as a failure.

  refusal-report : Maybe Tensor -> Text
  refusal-report (m) =
    when m
     is None -> "refused"
     is Just (t) -> "SERVED " & show (list-length (t.data))

Section: The Other Operations

 These are dumped rather than asserted against named constants, and the
 expected file is the assertion. Each line is hand-checkable, and the
 numbers below are what exact arithmetic gives at scale 1000:

   softmax over four equal values is four times 250, because a uniform
     distribution over four outcomes is a quarter each. An identity
     transport would answer the input instead, which is nothing like it.
   add is [1,2,3,4] + [2,2,2,2] = [3,4,5,6].
   mul is [1,2,3,4] * [2,2,2,2] = [2,4,6,8].
   transpose of [[1,2],[3,4]] is [[1,3],[2,4]], so the middle two values
     swap and an identity answers the input unchanged.
   scale by 2.0 doubles.
   layer norm over [1,2,3,4] has mean 2.5 and population sd 1.118, giving
     -1.342, -0.447, 0.447, 1.342. It arrives a unit short at the ends
     (-1341, 1341) because the decoder loses low bits above 1.0, which is
     the same rounding the tolerance above exists for.
   GROUP NORM OVER THE SAME INPUT WITH TWO GROUPS IS THE CONTROL FOR THE
     GROUP COUNT: each pair normalises against its own mean, so every
     value lands one sd away and the answer is plus or minus one, which
     arrives as 999. An implementation that ignored the grouping would
     return the layer norm numbers above instead, and the two are nowhere
     near each other -- that is the whole point of running both on the
     same input.
   silu and gelu are both zero at zero and both near 10.0 at 10.0, which
     is what pins them as S-shaped rather than linear or constant. They
     are not identical there -- silu decodes to 9992 and gelu to 10000 --
     so the two ops are also distinguishable from each other.
   upsample doubles a 1x2 into a 2x4, so each value appears twice across
     and the row appears twice down.

  dump : Maybe Tensor -> Text
  dump (m) =
    when m
     is None -> "NONE"
     is Just (t) -> show (t.rows) & "x" & show (t.cols) & " " & vals (t.data) 0 (list-length (t.data)) ""

  vals : List Integer, Integer, Integer, Text -> Text
  vals (xs) (i) (n) (acc) =
    if i >= n then acc
    else let sep = if i > 0 then " " else ""
    in vals xs (i + 1) n (acc & sep & show (list-at xs i))

  quad : Tensor
  quad = Tensor { data = [1000, 2000, 3000, 4000], rows = 2, cols = 2 }

  twos : Tensor
  twos = Tensor { data = [2000, 2000, 2000, 2000], rows = 2, cols = 2 }

  zeros : Tensor
  zeros = Tensor { data = [0, 0, 0, 0], rows = 2, cols = 2 }

  extremes : Tensor
  extremes = Tensor { data = [0, 10000], rows = 1, cols = 2 }

  strip : Tensor
  strip = Tensor { data = [1000, 2000], rows = 1, cols = 2 }

 conv1d, max-pool and clamp fit the doorbell's shape header with no
 protocol change, and each case is chosen so an identity or a wrong
 parameter answers something the expected file does not hold:

   conv1d slides kernel [1,2] over [1,2,3,4] at stride 1, valid, no flip,
     so out[o] = in[o] + 2*in[o+1]: 1+4, 2+6, 3+8 = 5, 8, 11. None of
     those is an input, and a flipped kernel or a wrong stride misses.
   max-pool takes the max of each stride-2 window of width 2 over
     [1,3,2,5,4,0]: max(1,3), max(2,5), max(4,0) = 3, 5, 4. An identity
     answers the six inputs; a stride-1 pool answers five values.
   clamp holds [1,4,5,9] to [3,7]: the ends move to the bounds and the
     interior passes, giving 3, 4, 5, 7. An identity leaves 1 and 9; a
     clamp that ignored its bounds would too.

  conv-in : Tensor
  conv-in = Tensor { data = [1000, 2000, 3000, 4000], rows = 1, cols = 4 }

  conv-ker : Tensor
  conv-ker = Tensor { data = [1000, 2000], rows = 1, cols = 2 }

  pool-in : Tensor
  pool-in = Tensor { data = [1000, 3000, 2000, 5000, 4000, 0], rows = 1, cols = 6 }

  clamp-in : Tensor
  clamp-in = Tensor { data = [1000, 4000, 5000, 9000], rows = 1, cols = 4 }

 conv2d carries eight shape fields in a header of its own, so it also
 tests that the alternate header reaches the host. A 2x2 all-ones kernel
 over the 3x3 [[1,2,3],[4,5,6],[7,8,9]] at stride 1, valid, sums each
 2x2 window: 1+2+4+5, 2+3+5+6, 4+5+7+8, 5+6+8+9 = 12, 16, 24, 28. None of
 those is an input value, so an identity or a dropped kernel term misses.

  conv2d-in : Tensor
  conv2d-in = Tensor { data = [1000, 2000, 3000, 4000, 5000, 6000, 7000, 8000, 9000], rows = 3, cols = 3 }

  conv2d-ker : Tensor
  conv2d-ker = Tensor { data = [1000, 1000, 1000, 1000], rows = 2, cols = 2 }

Section: Entry

  opening : [Console] Integer
  opening = act
    print-line-uni ("matmul: " & mm-report (gpu-bridge-matmul bridge mat-a mat-b))
    print-line-uni ("relu: " & relu-report (gpu-bridge-relu bridge relu-in))
    print-line-uni ("matmul-32x32: " & big-report (gpu-bridge-matmul bridge square square))
    print-line-uni ("mis-shaped: " & refusal-report (gpu-bridge-matmul bridge mis-shaped mis-shaped))
    print-line-uni ("softmax: " & dump (gpu-bridge-softmax bridge zeros))
    print-line-uni ("add: " & dump (gpu-bridge-elemwise-add bridge quad twos))
    print-line-uni ("mul: " & dump (gpu-bridge-elemwise-mul bridge quad twos))
    print-line-uni ("transpose: " & dump (gpu-bridge-transpose bridge quad))
    print-line-uni ("scale: " & dump (gpu-bridge-scale bridge quad 2000))
    print-line-uni ("layer-norm: " & dump (gpu-bridge-layer-norm bridge quad))
    print-line-uni ("group-norm: " & dump (gpu-bridge-group-norm bridge quad 2))
    print-line-uni ("silu: " & dump (gpu-bridge-silu bridge extremes))
    print-line-uni ("gelu: " & dump (gpu-bridge-gelu bridge extremes))
    print-line-uni ("upsample: " & dump (gpu-bridge-upsample2x bridge strip 1 1 2))
    print-line-uni ("conv1d: " & dump (gpu-bridge-conv1d bridge conv-in conv-ker 1))
    print-line-uni ("max-pool: " & dump (gpu-bridge-max-pool bridge pool-in 2 2))
    print-line-uni ("clamp: " & dump (gpu-bridge-clamp bridge clamp-in 3000 7000))
    print-line-uni ("conv2d: " & dump (gpu-bridge-conv2d bridge conv2d-in conv2d-ker 1 3 3 1 2 2 1 0))
    let r = 0 in r
  end

